<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neuschwanstein Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1869–1886</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neuschwanstein Castle is a 19th-century palace built for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a> on a rugged hill above the village of Hohenschwangau near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a>. Begun in 1869 as a romantic imitation of a medieval castle and inspired by the operas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>, it was still unfinished at the king's death in 1886. Opened to the public weeks later, it has become one of the most visited sights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neuschwanstein-castle.jpg" alt="Neuschwanstein Castle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Neuschwanstein_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
